Build notes
===========

Vendored Rust sources
---------------------
The build runs with CARGO_NET_OFFLINE and takes every crate from directory
sources under debian/, so `dpkg-buildpackage` needs no network:

  debian/vendor/                    crates.io
  debian/vendor-ffier-0.2.0rc1/     ffier at tag 0.2.0rc1
  debian/vendor-ffier-v0.2.0-rc2/   ffier at tag v0.2.0-rc2

The workspace pins two commits of the ffier repository (src/libkrun uses
v0.2.0-rc2, init and bindings use 0.2.0rc1). Both declare version 0.2.0, and a
vendor directory cannot hold two packages with the same name and version, which
is why each commit has its own directory source.

After changing any Cargo.toml or Cargo.lock, run `debian/update-vendor.sh`
(needs network) and commit the result. debian/cargo-home/config.toml maps the
sources and is read through CARGO_HOME, which debian/rules sets; the child
`cargo` that init-blob would spawn inherits it.

The trees hold no compiled objects: `debian/update-vendor.sh` deletes every
`*.a`, `*.la`, `*.o` and `*.so` after `cargo vendor` and drops the matching
entries from each crate's `.cargo-checksum.json`, which cargo accepts and which is
what dh-cargo's per-crate checksum manifests do. The objects come from crates
that only build for Windows and wasm targets (`winapi`'s MinGW import libraries,
`wit-bindgen`'s prebuilt C-ABI runtime), and dpkg-source would omit them from the
source package regardless, since its default ignore list excludes those
suffixes from everything under debian/ and cannot be shortened. Pruning them
keeps the vendored tree, the source package and the build graph in agreement.

The vendored trees still contain files that dpkg-source classifies as binary -
anything whose first 4 KiB hold a NUL byte, such as test fixtures and the images
in rutabaga_gfx - so `debian/source/include-binaries` lists those.
`debian/update-vendor.sh` regenerates the list together with the trees, so the
two cannot drift.

dh-cargo is not usable here: the workspace depends on a git repository that is
not in the archive, so the build drives cargo through the upstream Makefile
instead of through a Debian-patched Cargo.lock.

Rust toolchain
--------------
libkrun needs rustc 1.88: the sources use let-chains and `[T]::as_chunks`, which
were stabilised in that release. Debian ships a single toolchain under the plain
`cargo`, `rustc` and `rustfmt` names. Ubuntu additionally ships
parallel-installable toolchains whose binaries carry a version suffix, and its
plain names can be older than libkrun requires - on Ubuntu 25.10 the plain ones
are 1.85.1, while rustc-1.88, cargo-1.88 and rustfmt-1.88 are in
questing-updates/universe. Build-Depends therefore names the versioned packages
first, `cargo-1.88 | cargo (>= 1.88)` and the matching pairs, and debian/rules
builds a debian/rust-bin directory holding `cargo`, `rustc` and `rustfmt` links
to the versioned toolchain and puts it at the front of PATH. The upstream
Makefile, init/init-blob/build.rs and the ffier binding generator all invoke the
plain names.

rustfmt is a build dependency because bindings/libkrun-via-cdylib-weak
regenerates the Rust client during the build, and ffier-gen-rust-client runs
`rustfmt --edition 2024` over the generated code.

Guest init binary
-----------------
The guest PID 1 binary is embedded in libkrun_init.so. Upstream cross-compiles
it for <arch>-unknown-linux-musl, which requires a rustup toolchain with that
target installed; Debian and Ubuntu ship Rust standard libraries only for the
host triple. debian/rules therefore builds init/init-binary for the host gnu
triple with `-C target-feature=+crt-static` and hands the result to the
init-blob crate through KRUN_INIT_BINARY_PATH. The resulting guest init is a
static PIE linked against glibc; it links no DNS, NSS or dynamic-loading code,
so there is no behavioural difference for PID 1.

GPU device model
----------------
The `gpu` feature pulls in rutabaga_gfx, which requires pkg-config
`virglrenderer >= 1.3.0` and fails to build against older releases. Debian
forky/sid ship 1.3.0; Ubuntu 25.10 ships 1.1.0. debian/rules probes the
installed version and builds the GPU device model only where it is new enough,
printing a warning when it is not. libvirglrenderer-dev is listed unversioned in
Build-Depends so that the source package is installable in both distributions.

`virgl_resource_map2` (`make VIRGL_RESOURCE_MAP2=1`) is not enabled. The code
path at src/devices/src/virtio/gpu/virtio_gpu.rs:863 calls
`Rutabaga::resource_map` and imports `RUTABAGA_HANDLE_TYPE_MEM_SHM`, and neither
exists in rutabaga_gfx 0.1.85 - the version Cargo.lock pins, the version
src/devices/Cargo.toml requires, and the newest release on crates.io. Building
the generic variant with that feature fails with E0432 and E0599. The GPU device
model itself builds and packages correctly against virglrenderer 1.3.0.

Source package
--------------
The source format is 3.0 (quilt), so `dpkg-buildpackage -S` needs an upstream
tarball named after the upstream part of the version:

  libkrun_2.0.0~dev1.orig.tar.xz

Upstream has no 2.0.0 release yet, so that tarball is a snapshot of the git tree
with the `debian/` directory removed, for example
`git archive <commit> | tar -x -C libkrun-2.0.0~dev`, then `tar -cJf` that
directory. The vendor trees travel in the .debian.tar.xz because they live under
debian/, which is also why the binary fixture files appear in
debian/source/include-binaries.

Tests
-----
`make test` boots virtual machines and needs /dev/kvm plus libkrunfw, so
dh_auto_test is overridden to a no-op. There is no autopkgtest suite.

TEE variants (not packaged)
---------------------------
The AMD SEV and Intel TDX variants (`make SEV=1`, `make TDX=1`) do not build at
this upstream revision, so libkrun-sev2/libkrun-tdx2 and their -dev packages are
not shipped. `cargo build --release --features amd-sev,ffi` fails with:

  error[E0433]: failed to resolve: could not find `balloon` in `virtio`
    --> src/libkrun/src/api/mod.rs:150:27
  150 |   enum devices::virtio::balloon::FreePageReportingMode,

`src/devices/src/virtio/mod.rs` removes `virtio::balloon` when the devices crate
is built with its `tee` feature, which `amd-sev` and `tdx` enable through
`devices/amd-sev` and `devices/tdx`. The matching `#[cfg(not(feature = "tee"))]`
on the api entry is not applied to the code that `ffier::generate_bridge!`
emits for it, so the reference survives and fails to resolve. No job under
.github/workflows builds SEV or TDX, so CI does not catch this.

The variants can be packaged once that gating is fixed upstream: add the
libkrun-sev2, libkrun-sev-dev, libkrun-tdx2 and libkrun-tdx-dev stanzas to
debian/control with `Architecture: amd64`, an .install file per package, and the
corresponding `$(MAKE) $(MAKE_VARS) SEV=1 FFI=1` and `TDX=1` lines to the
dh_auto_build and dh_auto_install targets.
